            "text": "{\"study_topic_id\":\"study-e154d426b022180aa5ce\",\"overview\":{\"text\":\"Hoover Dam is a concrete arch-gravity dam in Black Canyon of the Colorado River, on the Nevada–Arizona border southeast of Las Vegas. Built as part of the federal Boulder Canyon Project, it was designed to help control floods, store water for farms and cities, and generate hydroelectric power. Construction began in 1931; the dam began impounding water in Lake Mead on February 1, 1935, and its last concrete was placed that May. The project reshaped the arid Southwest and became a landmark of large-scale engineering. First known as Boulder Dam, it was officially named Hoover Dam in 1947.\",\"source_urls\":[\"https://www.nps.gov/articles/nevada-and-arizona-hoover-dam.htm\",\"https://www.usbr.gov/history/hoover.html\",\"https://www.usbr.gov/lc/hooverdam/history/articles/chrono.html\"]},\"key_facts\":[{\"text\":\"The dam stands in Black Canyon, where the Colorado River forms the Nevada–Arizona border.\",\"source_urls\":[\"https://www.usbr.gov/history/hoover.html\"]},{\"text\":\"It is an arch-gravity dam, 726 feet above the riverbed; its concrete base is 660 feet thick.\",\"source_urls\":[\"https://www.nps.gov/articles/nevada-and-arizona-hoover-dam.htm\"]},{\"text\":\"The dam began holding back water to form Lake Mead on February 1, 1935. Its last concrete was placed May 29, 1935; President Franklin D. Roosevelt dedicated it that September.\",\"source_urls\":[\"https://www.usbr.gov/lc/hooverdam/history/articles/chrono.html\"]},{\"text\":\"Its planned roles included flood control, water storage for farms and cities, and hydroelectric power generation.\",\"source_urls\":[\"https://www.nps.gov/articles/nevada-and-arizona-hoover-dam.htm\"]},{\"text\":\"Congress officially designated it Hoover Dam in 1947, after it had also been called Boulder Dam.\",\"source_urls\":[\"https://www.usbr.gov/lc/hooverdam/history/articles/chrono.html\",\"https://www.nps.gov/articles/nevada-and-arizona-hoover-dam.htm\"]}],\"sources\":[{\"title\":\"Nevada and Arizona: Hoover Dam\",\"url\":\"https://www.nps.gov/articles/nevada-and-arizona-hoover-dam.htm\",\"publisher\":\"National Park Service\",\"retrieved_at\":\"2026-09-24T18:36:57.747384+00:00\",\"supports\":\"Dam type and dimensions; its planned water, flood-control, and power roles; the history of the Boulder Dam name and 1947 official naming; its regional significance.\"},{\"title\":\"Hoover Dam\",\"url\":\"https://www.usbr.gov/history/hoover.html\",\"publisher\":\"U.S. Bureau of Reclamation\",\"retrieved_at\":\"2026-09-24T18:36:57.747384+00:00\",\"supports\":\"Location in Black Canyon on the Arizona–Nevada state line.\"},{\"title\":\"Hoover Dam: Chronology\",\"url\":\"https://www.usbr.gov/lc/hooverdam/history/articles/chrono.html\",\"publisher\":\"U.S. Bureau of Reclamation\",\"retrieved_at\":\"2026-09-24T18:36:57.747384+00:00\",\"supports\":\"Construction contract and start, Lake Mead impoundment, placement of the final concrete, dedication, and the 1947 official naming.\"}],\"research_queries\":[\"site:nps.gov Hoover Dam history construction 1935 Lake Mead\",\"site:usbr.gov Hoover Dam arch gravity dam Colorado River history\",\"site:usbr.gov Hoover Dam facts Lake Mead Arizona Nevada\"],\"notes\":\"All three cited pages were opened and read. The overview distinguishes the start of water impoundment and placement of the last concrete in 1935 from the dam's dedication later that year.\",\"unresolved_concerns\":[],\"self_check_complete\":true}"
